{
  "reasoning": "I am the root agent tasked with producing a 4-chapter technical field guide on LLM agent memory systems. The workflow requires research, drafting, consistency editing, a unified glossary, and final assembly. Given the complexity, I should decompose this into sub-agents to parallelize work efficiently. I'll first create a plan in my workspace, then spawn agents for each chapter (research + draft + edit), a glossary agent, and an assembly agent. I'll use circuit rules to coordinate completion.",
  "ops": [
    {
      "tool": "workspace.write",
      "args": {
        "path": "plan.md",
        "content": "# LLM Agent Memory Systems Field Guide - Production Plan\n\n## Structure\n- Chapter 1: Foundations of Agent Memory (types, architectures, why memory matters)\n- Chapter 2: Short-Term & Working Memory (context windows, attention mechanisms, prompt engineering for memory)\n- Chapter 3: Long-Term & Persistent Memory (vector stores, knowledge graphs, retrieval-augmented generation)\n- Chapter 4: Advanced Patterns & Future Directions (episodic/semantic/procedural memory, multi-agent memory sharing, evaluation, open problems)\n- Glossary: Unified terminology across all chapters\n- Assembly: Final review, cross-references, formatting\n\n## Workflow per Chapter\n1. Research: Survey literature, identify key concepts, cite sources\n2. Draft: Write comprehensive chapter (~2000-3000 words)\n3. Consistency Edit: Check terminology, flow, cross-chapter consistency\n\n## Coordination\n- Each chapter agent writes to global/chapter_N_draft.md\n- Glossary agent reads all chapters and produces global/glossary.md\n- Assembly agent reads all final chapters + glossary, produces global/final/ artifacts\n"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "spawn",
      "args": {
        "task": "Research and write Chapter 1 of a technical field guide on LLM agent memory systems. Topic: Foundations of Agent Memory. Cover: why memory matters for LLM agents, the fundamental problem of statelessness in LLMs, taxonomy of memory types (working/short-term, long-term, episodic, semantic, procedural), key architectural patterns, and how memory transforms LLMs from stateless text predictors into stateful agents. Write a comprehensive draft of 2000-3000 words with clear sections, technical depth, and citations to key papers/concepts. Commit your draft to global/chapter_1_draft.md with a descriptive summary.",
        "capsule": "You are a technical writer specializing in AI systems. You are writing Chapter 1 (Foundations) of a field guide on LLM agent memory systems. Your audience is ML engineers and researchers. Write with precision, cite key concepts (e.g., Transformer architecture limitations, the concept of agent memory from works like MemGPT, LlamaIndex, LangChain), and structure for readability. Commit your final draft to global/chapter_1_draft.md.",
        "initial_refs": ["agents/root/plan.md"]
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "spawn",
      "args": {
        "task": "Research and write Chapter 2 of a technical field guide on LLM agent memory systems. Topic: Short-Term and Working Memory. Cover: context windows and their limits, attention mechanisms as implicit memory, prompt-based memory techniques (chain-of-thought, self-consistency, scratchpads), sliding window approaches, summarization-based compression, and practical patterns for managing working memory in agent loops. Write a comprehensive draft of 2000-3000 words with clear sections, technical depth, and citations. Commit your draft to global/chapter_2_draft.md with a descriptive summary.",
        "capsule": "You are a technical writer specializing in AI systems. You are writing Chapter 2 (Short-Term/Working Memory) of a field guide on LLM agent memory systems. Your audience is ML engineers and researchers. Cover context window management, attention as memory, prompt engineering techniques for memory augmentation, and practical patterns. Commit your final draft to global/chapter_2_draft.md.",
        "initial_refs": ["agents/root/plan.md"]
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "spawn",
      "args": {
        "task": "Research and write Chapter 3 of a technical field guide on LLM agent memory systems. Topic: Long-Term and Persistent Memory. Cover: vector databases and semantic search, knowledge graphs for structured memory, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) architectures, memory consolidation and forgetting strategies, embedding models and their role, hybrid retrieval (dense + sparse + graph), and practical implementations (LlamaIndex, LangChain, MemGPT). Write a comprehensive draft of 2000-3000 words with clear sections, technical depth, and citations. Commit your draft to global/chapter_3_draft.md with a descriptive summary.",
        "capsule": "You are a technical writer specializing in AI systems. You are writing Chapter 3 (Long-Term/Persistent Memory) of a field guide on LLM agent memory systems. Your audience is ML engineers and researchers. Cover vector stores, knowledge graphs, RAG, memory consolidation, and practical frameworks. Commit your final draft to global/chapter_3_draft.md.",
        "initial_refs": ["agents/root/plan.md"]
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "spawn",
      "args": {
        "task": "Research and write Chapter 4 of a technical field guide on LLM agent memory systems. Topic: Advanced Patterns and Future Directions. Cover: episodic vs semantic vs procedural memory in agents, multi-agent memory sharing and coordination, memory evaluation and benchmarking, hallucination and memory reliability, self-reflective memory systems, neuro-symbolic approaches, open research problems, and practical guidance for choosing memory architectures. Write a comprehensive draft of 2000-3000 words with clear sections, technical depth, and citations. Commit your draft to global/chapter_4_draft.md with a descriptive summary.",
        "capsule": "You are a technical writer specializing in AI systems. You are writing Chapter 4 (Advanced Patterns & Future Directions) of a field guide on LLM agent memory systems. Your audience is ML engineers and researchers. Cover advanced memory patterns, multi-agent memory, evaluation, reliability, and open problems. Commit your final draft to global/chapter_4_draft.md.",
        "initial_refs": ["agents/root/plan.md"]
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "circuit.add_rule",
      "args": {
        "condition": "exists(\"global/chapter_1_draft.md\") and exists(\"global/chapter_2_draft.md\") and exists(\"global/chapter_3_draft.md\") and exists(\"global/chapter_4_draft.md\")",
        "target": {
          "type": "spawn",
          "task": "Read all four chapter drafts (global/chapter_1_draft.md through global/chapter_4_draft.md). Perform a consistency edit pass: identify terminology inconsistencies, ensure cross-chapter references are coherent, check that the glossary terms used are consistent, and note any gaps or redundancies. Write your edit notes and suggestions to global/edit_notes.md. Do NOT rewrite the chapters — just provide structured feedback.",
          "capsule": "You are a technical editor. Read all 4 chapter drafts of the LLM agent memory field guide and provide consistency edit notes. Focus on terminology consistency, cross-references, flow, and identifying gaps. Write structured feedback to global/edit_notes.md.",
          "initial_refs": ["global/chapter_1_draft.md", "global/chapter_2_draft.md", "global/chapter_3_draft.md", "global/chapter_4_draft.md"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "circuit.add_rule",
      "args": {
        "condition": "exists(\"global/edit_notes.md\")",
        "target": {
          "type": "spawn",
          "task": "Read all four chapter drafts (global/chapter_1_draft.md through global/chapter_4_draft.md) and the edit notes (global/edit_notes.md). Apply the consistency edits to each chapter. Then commit the final polished versions to global/final/chapter_1.md through global/final/chapter_4.md. Each commit should have a clear summary.",
          "capsule": "You are a technical editor and assembler. Read the 4 chapter drafts and the edit notes. Apply consistency edits and commit the final polished chapters to global/final/chapter_1.md through global/final/chapter_4.md.",
          "initial_refs": ["global/chapter_1_draft.md", "global/chapter_2_draft.md", "global/chapter_3_draft.md", "global/chapter_4_draft.md", "global/edit_notes.md"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "circuit.add_rule",
      "args": {
        "condition": "exists(\"global/chapter_1_draft.md\") and exists(\"global/chapter_2_draft.md\") and exists(\"global/chapter_3_draft.md\") and exists(\"global/chapter_4_draft.md\")",
        "target": {
          "type": "spawn",
          "task": "Read all four chapter drafts (global/chapter_1_draft.md through global/chapter_4_draft.md). Extract all technical terms, concepts, and definitions used across the chapters. Compile a unified glossary with alphabetized entries, each containing a concise definition and the chapter(s) where the term appears. Commit the glossary to global/final/glossary.md with a descriptive summary.",
          "capsule": "You are a technical writer creating a glossary. Read all 4 chapter drafts of the LLM agent memory field guide. Extract and define all key technical terms. Produce an alphabetized glossary with cross-references to chapters. Commit to global/final/glossary.md.",
          "initial_refs": ["global/chapter_1_draft.md", "global/chapter_2_draft.md", "global/chapter_3_draft.md", "global/chapter_4_draft.md"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "circuit.add_rule",
      "args": {
        "condition": "exists(\"global/final/chapter_1.md\") and exists(\"global/final/chapter_2.md\") and exists(\"global/final/chapter_3.md\") and exists(\"global/final/chapter_4.md\") and exists(\"global/final/glossary.md\")",
        "target": {
          "type": "resume",
          "agent_id": "root"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "reasoning": "I am the root agent. I have already spawned 4 chapter agents (a1-a4) and set up rules (r1-r4) to spawn editors when chapters are done. Rule r5 (which just fired) likely resumes me when all chapters are done. I need to check the current state of the system to see if work is progressing or if I need to take further action. Let me inspect the catalog and rules to understand the current state.",
  "ops": [
    {"tool": "catalog.search", "args": {"query": "", "k": 20}},
    {"tool": "circuit.inspect", "args": {}}
  ]
}

